1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,306
If you're here, I'd guess something like this happened.

2
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:05,295
An AI feature landed on your roadmap.

3
00:00:05,295 --> 00:00:08,090
Maybe you asked for it, maybe it arrived from above.

4
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:12,552
And somewhere between the demo and the launch date you realised you had no reliable

5
00:00:12,552 --> 00:00:16,798
way to tell whether the thing actually works, and that everyone in the room was

6
00:00:16,798 --> 00:00:18,465
quietly waiting for you to say.

7
00:00:18,465 --> 00:00:20,507
That's not a gap in your intelligence.

8
00:00:20,507 --> 00:00:21,905
It's a gap in the toolkit.

9
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:26,205
The methods you were trained on assume software that behaves the same way twice.

10
00:00:26,205 --> 00:00:26,904
This doesn't.

11
00:00:27,454 --> 00:00:31,922
The résumé version is on screen if you want the specifics: years in AI product work,

12
00:00:31,922 --> 00:00:34,901
a computing degree with distinction, a year at Stanford.

13
00:00:34,901 --> 00:00:39,051
Read it once and move on, because that isn't actually the reason to trust what

14
00:00:39,051 --> 00:00:39,476
follows.

15
00:00:39,476 --> 00:00:43,945
Everything from here on is built to be checked, not taken on trust: a golden set you

16
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:48,147
can inspect, a pass rate you can recompute, a risk register you can argue with.

17
00:00:48,147 --> 00:00:52,137
Judge the course the same way you're about to learn to judge an AI feature.

18
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:54,110
Here's the concrete change.

19
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:58,274
Right now, the honest version of your status update is "it seems to work well".

20
00:00:58,274 --> 00:01:02,490
By the end of this course you'll be saying "ninety-four percent pass rate on our

21
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:06,865
golden set, three known failure modes, and here's the one I'd hold the launch for."

22
00:01:06,865 --> 00:01:10,765
You'll settle quality arguments with a number everyone can go and look at.

23
00:01:10,765 --> 00:01:15,087
You'll know what the feature costs per user before anyone writes code, rather than

24
00:01:15,087 --> 00:01:16,405
when the invoice arrives.

25
00:01:16,405 --> 00:01:20,832
And you'll have surfaced the legal and safety issues at kickoff, not in the week you

26
00:01:20,832 --> 00:01:21,886
were hoping to ship.

27
00:01:22,436 --> 00:01:25,144
Three commitments about how this course is built.

28
00:01:25,144 --> 00:01:26,471
First, you build things.

29
00:01:26,471 --> 00:01:31,113
There are twelve downloadable artifacts, and they're real work deliverables: a spec,

30
00:01:31,113 --> 00:01:33,711
an eval harness, a cost model, a risk register.

31
00:01:33,711 --> 00:01:36,032
You'll finish with a portfolio, not notes.

32
00:01:36,032 --> 00:01:39,348
Second, I've kept this deliberately light on specific tools.

33
00:01:39,348 --> 00:01:40,951
Tools churn every few months.

34
00:01:40,951 --> 00:01:41,946
Judgement doesn't.

35
00:01:41,946 --> 00:01:46,091
What you learn here will still be true after the next three model releases.

36
00:01:46,091 --> 00:01:48,026
Third, I've left the trade-offs in.

37
00:01:48,026 --> 00:01:51,618
Where this approach is expensive, or slow, or overkill, I say so.

38
00:01:51,618 --> 00:01:56,261
You're going to have to defend these ideas to a skeptical engineer, and you can't do

39
00:01:56,261 --> 00:01:57,587
that from a sales pitch.

40
00:01:58,137 --> 00:01:59,506
One ask before we start.

41
00:01:59,506 --> 00:02:00,476
Do the exercises.

42
00:02:00,476 --> 00:02:02,815
I know that's what every instructor says.

43
00:02:02,815 --> 00:02:07,492
But this material is skill, not information, and the difference between people who

44
00:02:07,492 --> 00:02:12,284
get real value here and people who get a vocabulary refresh is entirely whether they

45
00:02:12,284 --> 00:02:13,653
paused and did the work.

46
00:02:13,653 --> 00:02:18,331
The exercise two lectures from now takes five minutes and is the one I'd fight you

47
00:02:18,331 --> 00:02:18,502
on.

48
00:02:18,502 --> 00:02:19,186
Do that one.

49
00:02:19,186 --> 00:02:23,750
Next up: how to get through this efficiently, and what all twelve artifacts are.

50
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:27,401
Then we go straight into the idea this whole course is built on.
